      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the previous response; I will stick strictly to the technical variables you requested regarding pressure, temperature, and dwell time for your sublimation issue.\n\nRegarding your specific question on **4 bar of pressure**: For standard sublimation transfers, 4 bar (approximately 58 psi) is generally considered the standard operating pressure for most heat presses. However, for **high-density dark inks**, the ink particles are larger and require more force to penetrate the polyester fibers effectively. If your current setup is producing washed-out results with dark shades, 4 bar might be insufficient to force the ink deep enough into the fabric, especially if the fabric is thick or has a tight weave.\n\nYou should consider increasing your pressure incrementally. Many operators find success with **5 to 6 bar** for dark, high-opacity designs to ensure the ink is fully embedded. If you increase the pressure too much, you risk damaging the fabric or causing the ink to bleed into the lighter areas, so it is best to test in small increments (e.g., 0.5 bar steps).\n\nRegarding **dwell time**: You mentioned running for 40 seconds. For dark inks, the dwell time is often critical because the ink",
